text:
The Wall
The Wall is thousands of lines of generated diff (the list of lines a change added and removed), produced faster than anyone can review. If you merge it unread, the Blight spreads. If you try to read all of it, your attention thins and you approve things you did not understand. Both routes fail.
The way through is to refuse it whole: keep a review budget (only produce as much as you can honestly review today, because bulk low-quality output is called slop), ask for smaller pieces, ground each piece in a check, and pass on only work you have read yourself.
A human check can fail in two ways. Automation bias is trusting the machine’s output too readily. Rubber-stamping is approving without reading. An “approve each item” step at hundreds of items an hour turns into clicking through. A check that works:

shows what changed, at a volume a person can read;
offers approve, edit, veto and stop;
keeps a real decision point wherever results or other people are affected;
records the human step as part of the work’s provenance (the record of where a result came from).

1. What a cloud session is
A cloud session is a Claude Code session that runs on Anthropic’s servers, away from your laptop. Each one gets a fresh virtual machine (a computer simulated inside a bigger one, thrown away afterwards; Ubuntu 24.04 on x86_64) with a fresh clone (a full copy of a repository) of your code, made from its copy on GitHub. It keeps working after you close your laptop. When it is done, the work comes back as a branch, which you can turn into a pull request (a proposal on GitHub to merge that branch, with a place to review the changes).
Only committed files (changes you have saved into git history with git commit) travel, because the clone is made from GitHub. A committed CLAUDE.md (the instructions file Claude Code reads at the start of a session), the rules under .claude/ (the folder where a repository keeps Claude Code settings and instruction files), skills and agents come along. What stays behind:

your personal ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (the same kind of file, kept in your home folder), skills, agents and commands, and your user-level settings;
MCP servers (add-on connections that give the agent extra tools) you added at local or user scope;
.claude/settings.local.json;
interactive sign-ins such as AWS SSO (single sign-on, one shared login for many services).

Organisation-managed server settings do still apply.

The permission rules
The tool enforces permission rules, checking deny first, then ask, then allow.

Claude CodeOpenCode + Ollama


A teaching configuration:
{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": ["Bash(squeue *)", "Bash(sinfo *)", "Bash(sacct *)"],
    "ask":   ["Bash(sbatch *)", "Bash(srun *)", "Bash(salloc *)", "Bash(scancel *)"],
    "deny":  ["Bash(scancel -u *)", "Bash(scancel --user*)", "Bash(scancel --me*)"]
  }
}
An ask rule prompts even when a narrower allow rule matches. Ask and deny rules apply to every part of a compound command (&&, ;, |, loops, $(...)).
Bash rules are not a security boundary. Bash(rm *) does not stop /bin/rm or bash -c 'rm ...', and a scancel rule can be bypassed the same way. For real enforcement use a PreToolUse hook (the sandbox limits files and network and leaves Slurm commands alone). Treat the rules above as a prompt that keeps you in the loop.


OpenCode accepts allow, ask and deny, and an unmatched action asks. Its base policy begins with an allow-all rule, so sbatch runs without a prompt until you add a rule. Check your installed version’s permission docs, then add this to the permissions array in opencode.jsonc:
"permissions": [
  { "action": "shell", "resource": "sbatch *", "effect": "ask" }
]
Add similar rules for srun, salloc and scancel, and a deny for scancel -u * and scancel --me*. Rules are ordered, so check where they sit relative to the broad allow. As in Claude Code, a command that no rule matches goes through, and shell rules do not sandbox anything.

3. Point OpenCode at it
Install OpenCode, then connect inside it with /connect (a /command is typed inside the running tool):
curl -fsSL https://opencode.ai/v2/install | bash
If the install command errors, check the current OpenCode docs for your version.
OpenCode finds Ollama at http://127.0.0.1:11434 automatically. To spell it out, put this in ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json.
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "providers": {
    "ollama": { "settings": { "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1" } }
  }
}

4. Put the Pet on a leash
OpenCode’s default policy is allow everything, except reading .env files, which it asks about. Shell commands run with your user’s authority, and the shell is not a sandbox. Each rule says allow (run without asking), ask (prompt you first) or deny (never run). A safer start asks about everything first, then narrows. Rules are checked in order and the last match wins:
{
  "permissions": [
    { "action": "*", "resource": "*", "effect": "ask" },
    { "action": "shell", "resource": "git status *", "effect": "allow" },
    { "action": "shell", "resource": "git push *", "effect": "deny" }
  ]
}
